<!DOCTYPE precept [
    <!ENTITY date "2026-07-02">
]>


<section xml:id="sec-hidden-order" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Lens of Representation: Revealing Hidden Order</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
        A distinguishing feature between science and mathematics is the description of observations versus the description
        of structured logic. Yet, the most profound breakthroughs often happen when the observations and their corresponding
        systems are viewed from a new perspective that allows for their representation in terms of alternate forms of
        structured logic.
    </p>
    <p>
        This section will explore this profound relation between observed data and the structured logic used to
        represent them in terms of four primary representation lenses in which hidden order emerges.
    </p>

  </introduction>

  <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <alert>Hidden Order Through Frequency and Basis Change</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Transforming time-domain signals into frequency or scale domains reveals structure masked by noise or complexity.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><p><em>Fourier Transform</em>: Uncovers periodicity in chaotic signals.</p></li>
            <li><p><em>Laplace/Wavelets</em>: Reveals transient structures and scaling laws.</p></li>
            <li><p><em>Principal Component Analysis (PCA) / SVD</em>: Uncovers low-dimensional manifolds hidden in
            high dimensional data.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <alert>Hidden Order Through Geometric Symmetry</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Representing physical laws in coordinate-independent forms (like Geometric Algebra) reveals conserved quantities and symmetries.
        </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>